 },
 {
  "id": "text_UEdYgT3k",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 80,
  "top": 48,
  "width": 700,
  "height": 52,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:32px\"><strong>Why Computers Speak in Bits and Bytes</strong></p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "text_Vij37coe",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 80,
  "top": 98,
  "width": 700,
  "height": 46,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:16px\">Hardware has two reliable states — everything else is encoding</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "shape_rePehnSl",
  "type": "shape",
  "left": 60,
  "top": 165,
  "width": 430,
  "height": 150
 },
 {
  "id": "text_fBDXB13K",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 80,
  "top": 180,
  "width": 390,
  "height": 118,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:20px\"><strong>WHY two states?</strong></p><p style=\"font-size:16px\">• A switch is either on or off — no ambiguity</p><p style=\"font-size:16px\">• Voltage drifts; two levels stay distinguishable</p><p style=\"font-size:16px\">• So all data is encoded as 0s and 1s</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "shape_B6Nh3u7C",
  "type": "shape",
  "left": 510,
  "top": 165,
  "width": 430,
  "height": 150
 },
 {
  "id": "text_YNSgcReB",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 530,
  "top": 180,
  "width": 390,
  "height": 118,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:20px\"><strong>Switches → definitions</strong></p><p style=\"font-size:16px\">• Analogy: a row of light switches</p><p style=\"font-size:16px\">• <strong>Bit</strong> = one binary digit, 0 or 1</p><p style=\"font-size:16px\">• <strong>Byte</strong> = 8 bits grouped together</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "text_s2iqZON1",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 60,
  "top": 330,
  "width": 430,
  "height": 46,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:18px\"><strong>Example 1 — the doubling rule</strong></p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "latex_Xi-RKdih",
  "type": "latex",
  "left": 70,
  "top": 375,
  "width": 400,
  "height": 60
 },
 {
  "id": "text_z-NZljWq",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 60,
  "top": 440,
  "width": 430,
  "height": 46,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:15px\">Each extra bit doubles the number of patterns.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "text_NCWQdISf",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 510,
  "top": 330,
  "width": 430,
  "height": 46,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:18px\"><strong>Example 2 — numbering 100,000 users</strong></p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "latex_zBijXmU_",
  "type": "latex",
  "left": 520,
  "top": 375,
  "width": 400,
  "height": 60
 },
 {
  "id": "text_cIOmZyIp",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 510,
  "top": 440,
  "width": 430,
  "height": 46,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:15px\">1 byte is too small; 3 bytes are enough.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "shape_JTiPSSf5",
  "type": "shape",
  "left": 60,
  "top": 492,
  "width": 880,
  "height": 60
 },
 {
  "id": "text_yqRfNzkb",
  "type": "text",
  "left": 80,
  "top": 499,
  "width": 840,
  "height": 46,
  "content": "<p style=\"font-size:16px\"><strong>Watch out:</strong> bits carry no built-in meaning — interpretation does. Q: \"Why not ten states?\" → ten voltage levels are hard to tell apart reliably.</p>"
 }
]